WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.980 --> 00:00:05.459
And a common question when coming to Unreal Engine,

2
00:00:05.459 --> 00:00:07.379
it can sometimes feel a lot to think,

3
00:00:07.379 --> 00:00:09.259
okay, I know about all this data,

4
00:00:09.259 --> 00:00:12.019
but where do I use it and how do I use it?

5
00:00:12.019 --> 00:00:16.160
So there's a lot of blueprint communication going on here.

6
00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:21.160
You'll see different wires connecting to different elements

7
00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:23.460
and it can get a little bit overwhelming.

8
00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:25.879
So let's try and demystify some of this.

9
00:00:25.879 --> 00:00:30.879
So essentially you can put any data wherever you like

10
00:00:31.859 --> 00:00:35.119
you know, maybe we should caveat this.

11
00:00:35.119 --> 00:00:37.399
It definitely not is a one size fits all

12
00:00:37.399 --> 00:00:40.979
and maybe we shouldn't say that, but on the whole,

13
00:00:40.979 --> 00:00:43.200
yes, you can put the data wherever you like,

14
00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:46.640
but let's kind of set a few guiding principles

15
00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:48.960
of what might be good practice.

16
00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:53.000
So what are the things do I need to access this data?

17
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.159
Like the blueprint communication,

18
00:00:55.159 --> 00:00:58.119
like if the controller is connected to the character,

19
00:00:58.119 --> 00:01:01.119
then I can pass things along quite easily.

20
00:01:01.119 --> 00:01:02.520
But if there are two actors

21
00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:05.420
that aren't currently connected with each other,

22
00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:10.420
does it make accessing two bits of data accessible easy?

23
00:01:10.719 --> 00:01:11.719
Possibly not.

24
00:01:11.719 --> 00:01:15.000
So how long do I want this data to live for?

25
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.359
You remember that the game instance is spawned at runtime

26
00:01:18.359 --> 00:01:20.400
and then destroyed again.

27
00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.620
So if you want something to persist,

28
00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:26.040
maybe the game instance isn't the right place to put that.

29
00:01:26.079 --> 00:01:31.079
And what other context might the data accidentally end up in?

30
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:35.680
So, you know, if you're trying to store player lives

31
00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:38.599
and you put the player lives on the character,

32
00:01:38.599 --> 00:01:43.219
but then you also put it in maybe the game instance

33
00:01:43.219 --> 00:01:46.040
and then you have a class that's called player lives

34
00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:49.239
and you might start get accidentally overwriting

35
00:01:49.239 --> 00:01:51.879
other bits of information, things could get messy.

36
00:01:51.879 --> 00:01:53.719
So you kind of just want to think a little bit

37
00:01:53.719 --> 00:01:56.200
about your architecture when you start.

38
00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:59.519
Maybe asking yourself these three questions as you go

39
00:01:59.519 --> 00:02:03.920
and that will help you organize your information.

40
00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:08.919
So with the Pawn, for the duration of the Pawn,

41
00:02:09.919 --> 00:02:12.400
it lives up until the level is destroyed

42
00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:14.319
or destroyed by the game logic.

43
00:02:14.319 --> 00:02:16.400
So multiple Pawns can be used

44
00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:19.280
in one level and play session.

45
00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:24.080
And, you know, something that the Pawn stores

46
00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:26.039
is its capabilities and interactions.

47
00:02:26.039 --> 00:02:29.199
So, you know, can it run, walk or fly

48
00:02:29.199 --> 00:02:33.080
or can it interact with a door or something like this?

49
00:02:34.159 --> 00:02:35.439
These are the kinds of things

50
00:02:35.439 --> 00:02:38.520
that you want to put on the Pawn class.

51
00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:43.439
There's some useful ways to get the Pawn data

52
00:02:43.439 --> 00:02:45.689
and set the Pawn data.

53
00:02:45.689 --> 00:02:50.370
So you'll see quite a common event that's in red here

54
00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:54.169
on the image that's called event actor begin overlap.

55
00:02:54.169 --> 00:02:56.129
And this is, you know, a standard trigger

56
00:02:56.129 --> 00:02:59.569
inside of Unreal that detects something

57
00:02:59.569 --> 00:03:01.250
that overlaps the trigger.

58
00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:04.810
And you'll see that it says other actor with a blue pin.

59
00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:07.930
And it's asking what just overlapped me?

60
00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:10.930
Like, I am a trigger, what just overlaps me?

61
00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:12.810
And it checks to see if this is a Pawn.

62
00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:14.129
And if it's a Pawn,

63
00:03:14.129 --> 00:03:16.930
then you can get some of that Pawn's data.

64
00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:18.370
Maybe you want to say,

65
00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:21.090
is the Pawn currently swimming right now

66
00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:21.930
or something like this?

67
00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:24.169
I don't know what conditional check you want to do,

68
00:03:24.169 --> 00:03:29.180
but you have that information there to check against.

69
00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:31.419
You can also get the player Pawn

70
00:03:31.419 --> 00:03:33.699
and you can also get the controlled Pawn.

71
00:03:33.699 --> 00:03:38.139
So get, look, remember to look out for the player index

72
00:03:38.139 --> 00:03:40.699
because if there's multiple players

73
00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:44.900
then getting player Pawn might return many values.

74
00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:49.060
So zero will be just the first Pawn in the map.

75
00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:50.939
So player index zero.

76
00:03:50.939 --> 00:03:53.020
But if you get the controlled Pawn,

77
00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:55.180
you will always get the Pawn

78
00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:56.460
that the player is controlling.

79
00:03:56.460 --> 00:04:01.060
So worth keeping those things in mind.

80
00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:04.699
For the controller, again, the level of the controller

81
00:04:04.699 --> 00:04:07.379
lives until the level is destroyed.

82
00:04:07.379 --> 00:04:09.979
And it's not really expected to be destroyed

83
00:04:09.979 --> 00:04:13.620
because the controller essentially is the player.

84
00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:16.779
If you think when we're talking about the brain concepts,

85
00:04:16.779 --> 00:04:19.339
you don't really want the brain to get destroyed

86
00:04:19.339 --> 00:04:20.899
because it often outlays the Pawn.

87
00:04:20.899 --> 00:04:24.300
The Pawn might die and the Pawn might get destroyed,

88
00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:27.500
but you as the player still want to have a controller

89
00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:31.720
to even things like using the menu system

90
00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:34.740
that would still be run through some type of controller.

91
00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:39.740
So the Pawn might get killed, for example, in a game,

92
00:04:40.019 --> 00:04:41.699
but you might just possess a new Pawn

93
00:04:41.699 --> 00:04:43.980
and the controller would still be persistent.

94
00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:47.540
So this essentially relates to anything

95
00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:50.740
that is the player or an AI.

96
00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:51.819
Remember, it's a brain,

97
00:04:51.819 --> 00:04:54.740
so the AI would still need a controller.

98
00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:59.319
And so we might hold things like the score or kill count,

99
00:04:59.319 --> 00:05:01.680
or do you have health there?

100
00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:03.199
Again, you could store that in the character.

101
00:05:03.199 --> 00:05:04.620
I don't want to get too in the weeds

102
00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:06.000
of where you can store each things,

103
00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:09.279
but it is data agnostic in that respect.

104
00:05:09.279 --> 00:05:13.000
So just thinking about these concepts

105
00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:16.620
and how long the controller lives for

106
00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:19.139
is useful for when to store information.

107
00:05:19.139 --> 00:05:21.560
So to get the controller,

108
00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:23.360
we can just get the player controller,

109
00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:25.000
we can get controller.

110
00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:29.420
So that might be an AI controller that we want to get,

111
00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:32.680
and then you can get the owning player as well.

112
00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:35.040
So there's a few types of ways to get the player,

113
00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:38.339
well, the controller in general.

114
00:05:38.339 --> 00:05:39.939
Coming to the game mode.

115
00:05:40.259 --> 00:05:42.500
So like we said, the game mode is a single instance

116
00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:45.420
that exists before the level

117
00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:48.300
and persists as long as the level hasn't changed.

118
00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:49.720
So we can just get the game mode,

119
00:05:49.720 --> 00:05:51.300
very simply on the level blueprint,

120
00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:53.740
we can just do get game mode.

121
00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:58.740
And this is essentially useful for getting information

122
00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:01.259
for the level that's currently being played.

123
00:06:01.259 --> 00:06:04.139
It can be classed as more long-term data,

124
00:06:04.139 --> 00:06:07.779
which might span across multiple players,

125
00:06:07.779 --> 00:06:10.100
or be a single set of data

126
00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:12.220
that multiple entities need to get access to.

127
00:06:12.220 --> 00:06:15.060
Say there's a world clock or something like this

128
00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:18.490
that multiple players might need to know about.

129
00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:20.050
We've got the game instance.

130
00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:24.250
So the game instance spawns when you press play,

131
00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:27.810
and then as soon as you exit the level,

132
00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:29.290
the game instance will go away.

133
00:06:29.290 --> 00:06:30.810
So the game instance,

134
00:06:30.810 --> 00:06:33.509
you wouldn't really contain data

135
00:06:33.509 --> 00:06:35.689
that relates to a particular level or current events,

136
00:06:35.689 --> 00:06:37.410
it's just needed to store

137
00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:41.610
any kind of currently runtime information.

138
00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:44.370
And you can simply just get the game instance again,

139
00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:46.970
like the get game mode, just get game instance.

140
00:06:46.970 --> 00:06:50.490
And if you have a specific game instance

141
00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:53.569
that you've created, if you get the game instance,

142
00:06:53.569 --> 00:06:56.129
then you can cast to the name

143
00:06:56.129 --> 00:06:58.250
of your specific game instance

144
00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:01.730
to return some kind of variable from that,

145
00:07:01.730 --> 00:07:05.180
that game instance that you've created.

146
00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:08.540
So the HUD and HUD widgets, slightly different,

147
00:07:08.540 --> 00:07:12.579
but the HUD is spawned alongside the player controller,

148
00:07:12.579 --> 00:07:15.459
but it can be replaced at runtime by a function call.

149
00:07:15.459 --> 00:07:18.420
The HUD widget is in control of the player,

150
00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:20.660
but will be deleted on level close.

151
00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:24.019
So this is essentially the internal state

152
00:07:24.019 --> 00:07:29.019
of handling any UI elements, animations, current displays.

153
00:07:30.139 --> 00:07:32.259
You can reference it across actors,

154
00:07:32.259 --> 00:07:37.579
but again, you can store HUD widgets,

155
00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:43.819
like we said before in a porn or a player controller,

156
00:07:43.819 --> 00:07:47.579
you can just create a widget inside of these classes.

157
00:07:47.579 --> 00:07:49.579
So you don't necessarily just have to use this class,

158
00:07:49.579 --> 00:07:51.500
but if you are using the class,

159
00:07:51.500 --> 00:07:53.620
you can just right click, get HUD,

160
00:07:53.620 --> 00:07:58.060
and that will return the HUD that you might want to cast

161
00:07:58.060 --> 00:08:01.740
to that the player currently has set.

162
00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:04.540
The level blueprint, as we said,

163
00:08:04.540 --> 00:08:06.920
lasts for as long as the level is loaded

164
00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:10.339
and you might just have a very level specific scripts

165
00:08:10.339 --> 00:08:11.579
in which we already covered, you know,

166
00:08:11.579 --> 00:08:13.879
in the door example that I mentioned

167
00:08:13.879 --> 00:08:15.079
on the level blueprint.

168
00:08:16.589 --> 00:08:19.949
The level blueprint should listen for changes

169
00:08:19.949 --> 00:08:21.149
and orchestrate events.

170
00:08:21.149 --> 00:08:26.410
So any kind of, you know, gameplay scripts

171
00:08:26.930 --> 00:08:28.610
in that happens throughout the level,

172
00:08:28.610 --> 00:08:31.529
say the player does open this door

173
00:08:31.769 --> 00:08:33.929
and you want to listen for the door opening

174
00:08:33.929 --> 00:08:35.610
and then call a different event,

175
00:08:35.610 --> 00:08:40.149
something like that is why you'd use a level blueprint,

176
00:08:40.149 --> 00:08:44.409
but you can really get or set any specific data

177
00:08:44.409 --> 00:08:45.970
on the level blueprint.

178
00:08:45.970 --> 00:08:48.250
You wouldn't really want to take that approach.

179
00:08:48.250 --> 00:08:50.769
It's more just for the moment to moment scripting

180
00:08:50.769 --> 00:08:53.590
of a level blueprint, like in the examples we gave.

181
00:08:55.610 --> 00:08:58.529
You can make your own actor components.

182
00:08:58.529 --> 00:09:00.950
This is more advanced.

183
00:09:01.929 --> 00:09:03.370
So you don't need to do this.

184
00:09:03.370 --> 00:09:07.389
This would be more for maybe technical designers

185
00:09:07.389 --> 00:09:10.990
and technical artists and maybe more engineers.

186
00:09:10.990 --> 00:09:14.549
But when the data does need to be reusable

187
00:09:14.549 --> 00:09:16.850
and exist on several different actors,

188
00:09:16.850 --> 00:09:20.049
you can create blueprint actor components

189
00:09:20.049 --> 00:09:21.629
from the content browser.

190
00:09:21.629 --> 00:09:23.990
So that might be something you want to look into.

191
00:09:23.990 --> 00:09:26.230
It doesn't just need to be in the C++.

192
00:09:26.230 --> 00:09:28.870
You can create blueprint actor components

193
00:09:28.870 --> 00:09:33.110
and you can also make your own manager actors.

194
00:09:33.110 --> 00:09:35.710
So for example, if you're making a racing game

195
00:09:35.710 --> 00:09:38.129
and you want to store all the races

196
00:09:38.129 --> 00:09:41.350
and the current score and some kind of manager

197
00:09:41.350 --> 00:09:44.029
that manages the race,

198
00:09:44.029 --> 00:09:46.789
you would put this in just one per level

199
00:09:46.789 --> 00:09:49.470
and it just organizes everything into one place,

200
00:09:49.470 --> 00:09:51.029
which can be helpful.

201
00:09:51.029 --> 00:09:52.789
It can just be an empty blueprint class

202
00:09:52.789 --> 00:09:54.269
that is loaded on the level as well.

203
00:09:54.269 --> 00:09:58.250
So just thinking about the architecture there is very useful.

204
00:09:58.450 --> 00:10:01.250
And just to close out,

205
00:10:01.250 --> 00:10:03.230
if you are new to Unreal Engine,

206
00:10:04.250 --> 00:10:06.970
there is a great, great function to use.

207
00:10:06.970 --> 00:10:08.730
It's super secret.

208
00:10:08.730 --> 00:10:11.970
Nobody talks about it, but everyone talks about it.

209
00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:13.250
It's this node here.

210
00:10:13.250 --> 00:10:17.809
So you'll very quickly stumble upon this function

211
00:10:17.809 --> 00:10:20.029
as your Unreal journey unfolds.

212
00:10:20.029 --> 00:10:23.250
And it's a very useful kind of get all access of class.

213
00:10:23.250 --> 00:10:26.789
So, you know, you might be looking for a certain player,

214
00:10:26.789 --> 00:10:28.629
but you don't have a reference to the player.

215
00:10:28.629 --> 00:10:30.470
So you just want to get all access of class

216
00:10:30.470 --> 00:10:32.509
and you think, oh, does this one player exist?

217
00:10:32.509 --> 00:10:34.389
So I'll just get everything

218
00:10:34.389 --> 00:10:36.269
and then just return the first player

219
00:10:36.269 --> 00:10:38.389
and that will be my player and I'll do something with it.

220
00:10:38.389 --> 00:10:43.389
So you can do this for prototyping.

221
00:10:43.470 --> 00:10:44.830
It can be helpful,

222
00:10:44.830 --> 00:10:48.029
but it's a very heavy handed function to use.

223
00:10:49.820 --> 00:10:53.500
It can be not the most optimized way to do things.

224
00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:55.539
It can also cause bugs.

225
00:10:56.539 --> 00:10:58.019
So just use it sparingly,

226
00:10:58.019 --> 00:11:00.059
I think is the best recommendation there.

227
00:11:00.059 --> 00:11:04.059
It's similar to how Tic exists on a level blueprint

228
00:11:04.059 --> 00:11:06.659
or blueprints, should we say.

229
00:11:06.659 --> 00:11:09.379
And you can access Tic for a number of reasons,

230
00:11:09.379 --> 00:11:11.659
but you just want to use these kinds of things sparingly

231
00:11:11.659 --> 00:11:14.899
because especially if you're working in games in production,

232
00:11:14.899 --> 00:11:17.139
these are more heavy handed ways to do things

233
00:11:17.139 --> 00:11:20.700
and maybe better used in quick prototypes

234
00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:23.139
and just things that you're trying to get mocked up

235
00:11:23.139 --> 00:11:25.899
quickly just to demonstrate a behavior.
